========================================
POSITION: FIXED (Complete Notes)
========================================

Definition:
-----------
position: fixed positions an element relative
to the viewport (browser window).

The element remains fixed in the same position
even when the page is scrolled.

----------------------------------------

Key Characteristics:
--------------------
1. Removed from normal document flow.
2. Does not occupy space in the layout.
3. Positioned relative to the viewport.
4. Does NOT move when scrolling.
5. May overlap other elements.

----------------------------------------

How It Works:
-------------
When position: fixed is applied:

    position: fixed;

The element is taken out of the normal flow.
Other elements behave as if it does not exist.

Its position is controlled using:

    top
    right
    bottom
    left

Example:

    position: fixed;
    top: 10px;
    right: 20px;

This places the element:
- 10px from the top of the viewport
- 20px from the right of the viewport

----------------------------------------

Important Behavior:
-------------------

1. Scroll Behavior
   - The element does NOT move when the page scrolls.
   - It stays fixed on the screen.

2. Reference Point
   - Always relative to the viewport.
   - NOT relative to parent elements.

3. Overlapping
   - Since it is removed from flow,
     other elements may move up.
   - Overlapping can occur.

----------------------------------------

Difference from Absolute:
--------------------------
position: absolute
- Positioned relative to nearest positioned ancestor.
- Moves when the page scrolls.

position: fixed
- Positioned relative to viewport.
- Does NOT move when scrolling.

----------------------------------------

Common Use Cases:
-----------------
- Navigation bars
- Sticky headers
- Floating action buttons
- Chat icons
- Back-to-top buttons
- Popups / modals

----------------------------------------

Special Advanced Case:
----------------------
If a parent element has:

    transform
    filter
    perspective

That parent can create a new containing block.
In that case, the fixed element may behave
relative to that parent instead of viewport.

(This is an advanced CSS concept.)

----------------------------------------

Interview One-Line Answer:
---------------------------
position: fixed positions an element relative
to the viewport and keeps it fixed in place
even when the page is scrolled.
